 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>“Spring is a much more complicated collection to develop,” mused Henri Alexander Levy backstage before the Enfants Riches Déprimés show at Christie’s in Paris. “I think you have to really go deep inside to make it great. You can’t lean on coats or leather; it needs to be more romantic and emotional.”</p><p>For a brand that has made its name precisely on leather and a certain kind of LA attitude, that’s quite a realization. Romance, ERD-style, has a dark undertow: the show notes evoked “a mysterious unnamed figure who destabilizes a wealthy bourgeois family by seducing every member of the household.” Pasolini fans will recognize the late, great Terence Stamp in Teorema. There’s always a hint of trouble brewing.</p><p>With the first exits, though, the lineup seemed almost upbeat. Ladylike silhouettes and lean tailoring channeled the ’50s and ’60s in buttercream and brown, washed-out pink, gray, olive, and a royal blue pintucked shirt. Levy tends to burrow into film and music inspirations over fashion references; here, clean, modernist, and neorealist lines were informed by Pasolini’s Accattone as well as Teorema. The designer also said he’d been thinking about Miles Davis across the arc of his career—spare, sharp tailored silhouettes with inverse-pleated trousers evoked the musician in his early days and became more “torn down and fed up” as the show went on. Levy worked with textile artists to develop that faux-worn and spattered effect; he called the faded, checked trousers in look 21 “the divorced artist pant”—possibly coining a new fashion subcategory along the way.</p><p>In keeping with the brand’s name, he also looked to key moments in history: a ’70s-inspired ensemble—tan sweater, striped shirt, and leather pants with contrasting trim—was meant to recall John Paul Getty III “wandering around Rome a little bit confused.” Across the back of a leather jacket was printed Déclaration de Genève, a reference to the 1924 adoption of the first international text on children’s rights by the League of Nations.</p><p>The idea of art, its makers, and collectors ran through the show, which was staged around bronzes and sculptures by the late Catalan artist Antoni Tàpies. A paint-splattered British motoring jacket came with custom-woven ribbing. One model, in dark jeans and a cream jacket, strode out carrying a de Kooning drawing from Levy’s own collection (the designer said he figured that, this being Christie’s, he was in a safe space). Women’s wear was mostly prim and practically virginal, not counting the ultra-short shorts—in leather bearing the name, in Hebrew, of an iconic soft drink—or a black leather suit. ERD always makes room for a dramatic cape and a Morticia dress, but the best ideas here belonged to the guys. Like Levy in real life, they’re slowly evolving into something less outright angry and more arty, symbolized by neckerchiefs in lieu of neckties.</p><p>Levy can trip himself up on staging—models carrying knives felt decidedly OTT. More compelling were the heavy cast-silver baby dolls and pigs he made himself, treasures scored at the Puces and a bag set with a handful of rare pocket watches (yes, it’s a one-off, and beyond expensive). The ERD universe is expanding in more ways than one: These and other pieces are about to get a new home in Tribeca, which will sell art alongside clothes that are still romantic and a little bit damaged, but more calculated. This lineup seemed more interested in presence than posturing. That’s a step forward.</p></div>",
